@mirjak, agree that the two sections (Flow Session Awareness and Per-Flow Signaling) overlapped without a clear distinction. We've removed both. In their place is a single section whose purpose is operational, not a restatement of the protocol.  

The new section makes the point that applying throughput advice is stateless. The network element simply updates a traversing SCONE packet per its configured policy, whereas, the manageability functions this document covers (monitoring, logging, and conformance evaluation) do require maintaining per-flow context across monitoring periods. The protocol's definition of a flow is referenced, not repeated.

I believe this resolves the issue and I plan to close it by Friday, June 12. Please let me know before then if you would prefer it stay open. (Change is in PR #26 ).

@gorryfair wrote:

> I think this can only be examples - there must be millions of ways to do this, and it needs to say this is simply >some diverse ways to make this work, but I'm interested to see some examples of how this might work.

Please see PR #39 where text is added as examples, but this section is debated whether a separate section on Determining Throughput Constraints citing examples of how/what an operator may deploy to determine constraints. Argument is being made that will vary by each operator so listing anything in the section will never be exhaustive list. Also, there is a question whether "Operational Considerations" section is needed at all given the whole document is exactly that. Maybe keep the text for -02 and we can revisit if any text (sub-section on throughput constraints) should be retained with or without a "Operational Considerations" section

