@mirjak here is the simplified text. I'm pasting the pruned section below. I am also submitting a new PR exactly as I have it below including replacing "driven" with "decided".

**Frequency of Updates**:
For network operators, understanding that SCONE signaling is fundamentally decided by the application endpoint is critical. Because a SCONE Network Element relies entirely on these endpoint-generated packets to communicate throughput advice, operators should expect the frequency of traversing SCONE packets to vary significantly depending on the specific application type and its traffic characteristics. Consequently, network elements must be prepared to apply updates to traversing packets at highly variable, application-driven intervals rather than expecting a predictable or uniform signaling cadence from the network side.

@mirjak wrote:

> What I meant is covered by these "two monitoring periods": you need one period to be sure that the signal is conveyed and then you start you first actual monitoring period but you can start enforcement only at the end of that monitoring period.

Your breakdown of using the first period to convey the signal and starting enforcement only at the end of the second period makes perfect sense, and that is exactly the operational guidance text that can be added.

@gorryfair, Good catch. The phrase "normal update interval" was ambiguous since the protocol doesn't define a rigid network-side timer. We were referring to the periodic update cadence (e.g., 20-30 seconds) as now stated in the "Mitigating Packet Loss" section to ensure the signal doesn't expire. I have submitted an updated the text to explicitly reference that periodic update cadence so it's clear what the network element is waiting for. 

Also changed "throughput limit" in that sentence to "throughput advice". Here is the text for Dynamic Updates:
Target throughput advice can change dynamically due to access network handovers or changes in subscriber policy (e.g., reaching data thresholds). When such a change occurs, the network element can immediately update the next available traversing SCONE packet with the new throughput advice, rather than waiting for its next periodic update (such as the cadence established to mitigate packet loss). This immediate update minimizes the application's reaction time to the new network state.

@mirjak,  I have pruned this section to removed redundant protocol text. Rewrote it to focus strictly on the operational takeaway for the network operator, for e.g., "... because SCONE is stateless, handovers require no explicit teardown or state-transfer between network elements." 

Submitting a PR along with the section Presence of SCONE Network Element on The Data Path".

Let me know if this leaner, operations-focused version looks better to you

@mirjak. Valid point. Our intent was purely operational and advisory. We meant that network operators should ensure their policy configurations for SCONE limits and congestion control are consistent, so they don't give the application confusing or conflicting constraints. But, agree the current wording is just not helping to convey that. Here is the revised text. I am submitting a an update here with the whole section trying to incorporate all the above comments from you and @gloinul.

"In environments where both are present, congestion control manages the immediate dynamics of the bottleneck link, while SCONE informs the application of the maximum rate allowed by network policy. Network operators will benefit from ensuring that throughput advice policies and congestion control configurations are consistent within scoped deployments, to avoid providing conflicting feedback to applications."

Let me know if this get any better or worse :)

@ihlar 

Hi Marcus, I revisited your comment and have redone the paragraph and hopefully this addresses  your question on paragraph reads like marketing. I have now trimmed it to the substantive points and removed the user experience and business case language. It now states only that SCONE provides a standardized mechanism that avoids custom APIs and per-network integrations, and that applications can adapt to the advised bit rate rather than rely on policers or shapers.

I also retitled the section from "Standards Compliance" to "In-Band Signaling and Network Integration", since the content is really about SCONE operating in-band over the existing path rather than about compliance. Let me know if this reads better.

